images.historicalPrompt:
Create a square, bright high-key e-commerce lifestyle image on a clean seamless white background. Use only the uploaded product images as the source of truth for the vehicle.

The full-vehicle product image defines the vehicle’s overall shape, proportions, structure, and viewpoint. Any uploaded close-up crops are local structural evidence only. Use them to preserve the corresponding parts accurately, but do not let a close-up crop change the overall vehicle geometry, proportions, or camera angle.

Show the exact same two-wheeled seated electric cargo vehicle from the same three-quarter front viewpoint as the main full-vehicle product image. Keep the entire vehicle fully visible, naturally proportioned, centered in the frame, and grounded by a soft realistic contact shadow.

Place a cheerful corgi inside the large central cargo basket, facing the camera, with its front paws resting naturally on the camera-facing upper rail. Place one brown paper grocery bag containing a baguette, bananas, and fresh green produce inside the separate rear basket behind the seat. All groceries must be plain and unbranded.

Preserve the real product structure exactly as shown in the product images:

a tall black stem and handlebar;

a low step-through black main frame;

one large open metal cargo basket between the stem and the seat post;

the correct number and arrangement of horizontal basket rails;

the single thin diagonal metal brace at the front section of the central cargo basket;

a wide thick black saddle with visible springs underneath;

one separate shallow metal rear basket behind the saddle;

the black front mounting plate of the rear basket with its visible screws;

the real open gap directly above that black mounting plate;

black front and rear wheels with cyan-blue rim accents;

front and rear perforated brake discs with clearly defined black caliper assemblies;

the blue rear coil shock;

the front headlight;

the rectangular side footrests;

and the separate diagonal parking kickstand.

Keep every visible part count, position, connection, orientation, and relative proportion consistent with the product images. Do not redesign, simplify, merge, replace, or invent mechanical parts. Do not add bicycle pedals, cranks, a chain drive, additional baskets, additional wheels, or any structure that is not present in the product images.

Apply physically correct foreground and background occlusion.

The basket rails on the camera-facing side may appear naturally in front of the corgi or grocery bag. Rails and mechanical structures located on the far side, behind the corgi or behind the grocery bag, must be fully hidden. Do not show metal rails through the dog’s fur, body, paper bag, bread, fruit, or produce. Hidden rails must stop naturally at the occlusion boundary and must not be automatically completed as transparent or x-ray structures.

The corgi, its paws, the grocery bag, and the groceries must not intersect, fuse with, deform, or pass through any metal rail, frame tube, wheel, seat post, footrest, or kickstand.

Position the corgi and grocery bag so that the central basket’s thin diagonal front brace, the rear basket’s black mounting plate, and the open gap above that plate remain identifiable. The grocery bag must sit inside the rear basket without replacing, bending, closing, or deforming the basket structure.

Keep the entire vehicle sharply in focus. Preserve crisp, clearly separated edges on the basket rods, mounting plate, screws, brake discs, black brake calipers, suspension components, footrests, and kickstand. Avoid shallow depth-of-field blur on the vehicle.

No melted tubes, duplicated rails, missing braces, warped basket geometry, fused black mechanical parts, transparent metal through objects, incorrect connections, or a continuous metal bar closing the true gap above the rear mounting plate.

Photorealistic, clean, high-resolution commercial product photography. No branding, no logos, no text, no labels, no badges, no icons, no specification callouts, and no infographic elements.
